// heartsim tutorial: the frontal-plane QRS axis. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-app/src/tutorial.rs.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "cardiac_axis",
    title: "Cardiac axis",
    summary: "What the electrical axis is, how to estimate it from leads I and aVF, and what left and right axis deviation look like.",
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "What is the axis?",
            markdown: r#"
While the ventricles depolarise, millions of cells add up to one **electrical vector** that changes
direction from moment to moment. Averaged over the QRS complex it points in one main direction:
the **mean QRS axis**.

The six limb leads look at the heart in the **frontal plane** from six directions, 30° apart
(the *hexaxial* reference system shown on the circle):

| Lead | Angle |
|------|-------|
| I    | 0°    |
| II   | +60°  |
| III  | +120° |
| aVF  | +90°  |
| aVL  | −30°  |
| aVR  | −150° |

A lead records an upright QRS when the axis points towards its positive pole, and a negative QRS
when the axis points away from it. The normal adult axis lies between **−30° and +90°**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(AxisTrainer)),
                Ui(SetQrsAxis(60.0)),
            ],
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Leads I and aVF",
            markdown: r#"
Leads I (0°) and aVF (+90°) are at right angles, so together they divide the circle into four
quadrants. Look at the net direction of the QRS in each:

* **I up, aVF up**: between 0° and +90°, a normal axis.
* **I up, aVF down**: between 0° and −90°. Look at lead II to decide: if II is also mainly
  negative, the axis is beyond −30° (**left axis deviation**).
* **I down, aVF up**: between +90° and 180°, **right axis deviation**.
* **I down, aVF down**: between −90° and 180°, an **extreme** ("north-west") axis.

Drag on the circle to rotate the axis and watch leads I and aVF change.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(SetQrsAxis(60.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Lead I is mainly positive and aVF mainly negative. Where does the axis lie?",
                choices: [
                    "Between 0° and −90°",
                    "Between 0° and +90°",
                    "Between +90° and 180°",
                    "Between −90° and 180° (extreme)",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Positive in I means towards 0°; negative in aVF means away from +90°, so the axis is in the upper-right quadrant of the circle, 0° to −90°. Lead II then separates normal (down to −30°) from left deviation.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Left axis deviation",
            markdown: r#"
The axis is now at **−60°**. Lead I is positive, aVF and lead II are negative.

A lead whose QRS is as positive as it is negative (**equiphasic**) lies at right angles to the
axis. This gives a second, finer method: find the most equiphasic limb lead and choose the
perpendicular direction that matches lead I.

Left axis deviation is typical of **left anterior fascicular block**; it is also seen with
inferior myocardial infarction and some forms of ventricular pre-excitation and pacing.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(SetQrsAxis(-60.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "With the axis at −60°, which limb lead is closest to equiphasic?",
                choices: ["I", "aVR", "aVF", "III"],
                answer: 1,
                explanation: "aVR lies at −150°, exactly 90° away from −60°, so the axis projects onto it as zero.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Right axis deviation",
            markdown: r#"
The axis is now at **+120°**. Lead I is negative and aVF positive.

Right axis deviation can be normal in children and in tall, slim adults. Otherwise it suggests
**right ventricular hypertrophy** or strain, lateral myocardial infarction, or **left posterior
fascicular block**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(SetQrsAxis(120.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "At +120°, which limb lead shows the largest upright QRS?",
                choices: ["I", "II", "III", "aVL"],
                answer: 2,
                explanation: "Lead III looks at the heart from +120°, so the axis points straight at its positive pole.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "The axis in a real conduction problem",
            markdown: r#"
The simulator now runs **left anterior fascicular block**. The anterior fascicle of the left bundle
no longer conducts, so the upper-left part of the left ventricle is activated late, through the
posterior fascicle. The late activation pulls the mean vector upward and to the left.

Look at the twelve leads: small q and tall R waves in I and aVL, small r and deep S waves in II,
III and aVF. The QRS is only slightly wider than normal.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("lafb")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which axis is typical of left anterior fascicular block?",
                choices: ["+60° to +90°", "−45° to −90°", "+90° to +180°", "0° to +30°"],
                answer: 1,
                explanation: "LAFB shifts the axis to −45° or beyond.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Practise",
            markdown: r#"
The **Axis Quiz** tab hides a random axis behind a set of limb leads: read the leads, click your
answer on the circle and compare. Aim for an error under 15°.

Press **Finish** to close the tutorial.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(AxisQuiz)),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
